[E0134] Error: a piece is written to a precision or in a radix, not both
   ╭─[ err/387_interpolation_options_refused/main.wip:8:36 ]
   │
 8 │     val both = "\(x, precision: 2, radix: 16)"
   │                      ────┬────     ──┬──  
   │                          ╰──────────────── a precision
   │                                      │    
   │                                      ╰──── and a radix
   │ 
   │ Note: a precision is the digits after a float's point, and a radix the base an integer is written in
───╯
[E0134] Error: a fill or an alignment needs a width
   ╭─[ err/387_interpolation_options_refused/main.wip:9:22 ]
   │
 9 │     val filled = "\(n, fill: '0')"
   │                      ─────┬─────  
   │                           ╰─────── no `width:`
   │ 
   │ Note: the fill makes up the text to the width, and the alignment says where
───╯
[E0134] Error: a piece of an interpolation takes `width`, `fill`, `align`, `precision`, `radix` and `upper`
    ╭─[ err/387_interpolation_options_refused/main.wip:10:25 ]
    │
 10 │     val unknown = "\(n, base: 2)"
    │                         ──┬─  
    │                           ╰─── not one of them
    │ 
    │ Note: `\(value, width: 4, fill: '0', align: .Right)` pads the text to a width; `precision: 3` writes a float with three digits after its point, `radix: 16` an integer in hexadecimal, and `upper: true` its digits above 9 in upper case
────╯
[E0134] Error: upper-case digits are a radix's
    ╭─[ err/387_interpolation_options_refused/main.wip:11:25 ]
    │
 11 │     val shouted = "\(n, upper: true)"
    │                         ──┬──  
    │                           ╰──── no `radix:`
    │ 
    │ Note: `\(byte, radix: 16, upper: true)` writes an integer in hexadecimal with `A` to `F`
────╯
